<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cheomseongdae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cheomseongdae is a stone tower at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, the capital of Silla, traditionally dated to the reign of Queen Seondeok in the seventh century. About nine metres tall, it has a bottle-shaped body of granite courses with a square window halfway up. It is often described as the oldest surviving astronomical observatory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, though its exact use is debated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cheomseongdae.jpg" alt="Cheomseongdae" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Basile Morin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cheomseongdae_Observatory_under_blue_sky_in_Gyeongju_South_Korea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Cheomseongdae, whose name means star-gazing tower, stands 9.17 m high and has three parts: a base, a curved cylindrical body and a square top. When seen from above it resembles the Korean character jeong, meaning well. The square base is 5.7 m wide and built from a single layer of 12 rectangular stones. The body is built from 365 pieces of cut granite in 27 tiers, a number said to symbolise the days of the year. Documents report different counts, and a survey of 1962 found 366 blocks, a difference that may come from a slab inside the top that cannot be seen from outside.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>A square opening on the south side, between the 13th and 15th tiers, is believed to have been an entrance reached by an external ladder, while a ladder inside may have given access to the top. There, two tiers of well-shaped stones form a frame, and it is presumed that an observation device was placed on it. The opening divides the body into 12 layers above and 12 below, which have been taken to stand for the 12 months and the 24 solar terms. The 12 stones of the base may also refer to the months. The tower's construction style parallels that of the Bunhwangsa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Cheomseongdae was built in the Silla period, in the reign of Queen Seondeok (r. 632 to 647). It was designated the country's 31st national treasure on 20 December 1962 and is part of the Gyeongju Historic Areas, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Its shape has remained unchanged for over 1,300 years, though it now tilts slightly to the north-east. The National Research Institute of Cultural Heritage has inspected it regularly since 1981, and since 2007 a system has measured its condition every hour, watching for cracks and movements of the foundation stones. The government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> owns it, and the Gyeongju municipal government manages it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cheomseongdae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Divine Bell of King Seongdeok, completed in 771, is one of the largest surviving bells in Korea, and the <b>Cheomseongdae</b> tower at Gyeongju, traditionally dated to Queen Seondeok's reign, is often described as the oldest surviving observatory in East Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/silla">Silla · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
